+# CLI Contract Extension: `import` and `export --format html`
+
+**Feature**: `002-import-html-export` | **Date**: 2026-11-16
+
+This document extends — and never redefines — the contract of
+[`001-bookmark-manager-cli/contracts/cli-contract.md`](../../001-bookmark-manager-cli/contracts/cli-contract.md).
+Everything in that document stays true: the same five commands, the same bytes, the same
+diagnostics, the same statuses (FR-015, FR-016). What follows is the whole of what is new.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation (unchanged)
+
+```text
+python -m bookmarks [--db PATH] COMMAND [ARGS...]
+```
+
+`--db` remains a global option that must appear **before** the command, and still defaults to
+`bookmarks.db` in the working directory. `src/` must be on `PYTHONPATH` (FR-019, `001:FR-026`).
+
+---
+
+## New command: `import FILE --format csv|json`
+
+Reads bookmarks from `FILE` and adds every one of them, then reports how many it added.
+
+```text
+python -m bookmarks [--db PATH] import FILE --format {csv,json}
+```
+
+| Argument   | Required | Meaning |
+| ---------- | -------- | ------- |
+| `FILE`     | yes      | Path of a single local file to read (FR-001) |
+| `--format` | **yes**  | `csv` or `json`; no default, no inference (FR-001, FR-008) |
+
+**stdout on success**: exactly `imported N` followed by one newline, and nothing else, where `N`
+is the number of bookmarks added. **Exit 0** — including when `N` is `0` (FR-002).
+
+```console
+$ python -m bookmarks import colleague.json --format json
+imported 2
+$ python -m bookmarks list
+1	https://docs.python.org	Python docs	docs,python
+2	https://example.com		
+```
+
+### Behaviour of a successful import
+
+| Rule | Requirement |
+| ---- | ----------- |
+| Each bookmark is added exactly as `add` would add it: the scheme rule applies, the title is stored verbatim, the labels are trimmed, emptied of blanks, lower-cased and de-duplicated | FR-003 |
+| Any `id` in the file is disregarded; new identifiers come from the store's own rule | FR-004 |
+| Bookmarks are added in file order, so their identifiers ascend in file order | FR-005 |
+| Importing into a non-empty store **appends**: nothing is replaced, renumbered or removed, and the new identifiers exceed every identifier previously issued | FR-004 |
+| Addresses are **not** de-duplicated: a bookmark whose address is already stored is added again with its own identifier | spec Edge Cases |
+| The store is opened exactly as every other command opens it, so an absent store file is created — and left holding no bookmarks — even when `N` is `0` | FR-002a |
+
+### Accepted file shapes
+
+Both are the shapes `export` writes, so `export` output imports without edits. The full rules
+live in [data-model.md](../data-model.md); the essentials:
+
+**`--format csv`** (FR-006, FR-006a)
+
+```csv
+id,url,title,tags
+1,https://docs.python.org,Python docs,"docs,python"
+2,https://example.com,,
+```
+
+- First record must parse to exactly `id`, `url`, `title`, `tags`, in that order. The comparison
+  happens **after** CSV parsing, so `"id","url","title","tags"` is equally valid.
+- Every later record must hold exactly four fields.
+- Standard quoting is honoured: a quoted field may contain commas, doubled quotes and line
+  breaks. `\n` and `\r\n` both terminate a record.
+- The fourth field is read by the same rules as `add --tags`.
+- A blank line is a record of zero fields and is a **failure**; the newline ending the last row
+  is not a blank record.
+- The header alone imports 0 and succeeds.
+
+**`--format json`** (FR-007)
+
+```json
+[
+  {"id": 1, "url": "https://docs.python.org", "title": "Python docs", "tags": ["docs", "python"]},
+  {"id": 2, "url": "https://example.com", "title": "", "tags": []}
+]
+```
+
+- One array of objects. Each object must carry `url` (string), `title` (string) and `tags`
+  (array of strings).
+- `id` is accepted and its value disregarded; any other key is a failure.
+- An empty array imports 0 and succeeds.
+
+**Both shapes**: read as UTF-8; one leading byte-order mark is discarded; invalid UTF-8 is a
+failure (FR-009). The declared `--format` is authoritative — the name, extension and contents of
+the file are never consulted, and there is no fallback to the other shape (FR-008).
+
+### Failures
+
+Every failure below writes a diagnostic to **stderr** naming the file and what was invalid and
+what was expected, writes **nothing to stdout**, adds **no bookmark**, and exits **2** (FR-010,
+FR-011, FR-018).
+
+| Cause | Example diagnostic |
+| ----- | ------------------ |
+| File absent, a directory, or unreadable | `bookmarks: cannot read missing.csv: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'missing.csv'` |
+| Bytes are not valid UTF-8 | `bookmarks: cannot read bad.csv: it is not valid UTF-8 ('utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xff in position 0: invalid start byte)` |
+| Malformed in the declared shape | `bookmarks: broken.json is not valid JSON: Expecting ',' delimiter: line 3 column 5 (char 42)` |
+| CSV header record wrong, or missing entirely | `bookmarks: wrong.csv: expected the header record id,url,title,tags but found name,link` |
+| CSV record with the wrong field count (a blank line is zero fields) | `bookmarks: short.csv line 4: expected 4 fields (id,url,title,tags) but found 3` |
+| JSON top level not an array | `bookmarks: one.json: expected a JSON array of bookmark objects but found an object` |
+| JSON element not an object | `bookmarks: list.json record 2: expected a JSON object but found a string` |
+| JSON record missing a required key | `bookmarks: gap.json record 2: missing the required key 'tags'` |
+| JSON record with an unrecognised key | `bookmarks: extra.json record 1: unrecognised key 'note' (expected only id, url, title, tags)` |
+| JSON `tags` not an array of strings | `bookmarks: tags.json record 1: expected 'tags' to be an array of strings but found a string` |
+| An address fails the scheme rule | `bookmarks: refusing to store 'ftp://x.example.com': a URL must begin with 'http://' or 'https://'` |
+| Missing `FILE`, missing `--format`, or an unsupported `--format` value | argparse's own usage diagnostic (FR-001) |
+
+Diagnostic wording is illustrative, not contractual; what is contractual is the stream, the exit
+status, and that the message names the file (where a file is at fault), what was invalid and what
+was expected. The scheme-rule message is the existing one from `001`, reused unchanged.
+
+**Atomicity** (FR-011): a failed import leaves the collection exactly as it was — not even the
+records preceding the first fault are added — the next bookmark saved afterwards receives the
+identifier it would have received had the import not been attempted, and if the store file did
+not exist beforehand it is **not created**.
+
+**Store failures are separate**: an unusable store (missing parent directory, a file that is not
+a bookmark database) exits **1** with the existing diagnostic and leaves the file untouched,
+exactly as for every other command. A file problem is never reported as 1; `import` never
+exits 3 (FR-017).
+
+---
+
+## Extended command: `export --format {csv,html,json}`
+
+`export` gains a third value. **`csv` and `json` output is byte-for-byte unchanged** (FR-015,
+SC-006). An unsupported value still produces argparse's diagnostic on stderr and exit **2**.
+
+### `export --format html` (FR-012, FR-013, FR-014)
+
+Prints the whole collection in ascending identifier order as one page, exit **0**:
+
+```html
+<!DOCTYPE html>
+<html>
+<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Bookmarks</title></head>
+<body>
+<ul>
+<li><a href="URL">TITLE</a> <small>TAGS</small></li>
+</ul>
+</body>
+</html>
+```
+
+- The five opening lines and three closing lines are **fixed** and emitted verbatim.
+- One `<li>` line per bookmark, in ascending identifier order. The identifier itself does not
+  appear on the page.
+- `TAGS` is the bookmark's labels joined by `,` in alphabetical order, **no spaces** around the
+  commas. A bookmark with no labels still has the `<small>` element, holding nothing.
+- An empty collection prints the same page with **no** `<li>` line between `<ul>` and `</ul>`.
+- Every line, including the last, ends with a single `\n`; the page ends with exactly one
+  trailing newline. Non-ASCII characters are written literally as UTF-8, never escaped.
+
+Worked example — a store holding `https://docs.python.org` / `Python docs` / `python,docs`, and
+`https://example.com` / no title / no labels:
+
+```html
+<!DOCTYPE html>
+<html>
+<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Bookmarks</title></head>
+<body>
+<ul>
+<li><a href="https://docs.python.org">Python docs</a> <small>docs,python</small></li>
+<li><a href="https://example.com"></a> <small></small></li>
+</ul>
+</body>
+</html>
+```
+
+#### Escaping: a closed set of four substitutions
+
+| Character | `href` value | Title and labels |
+| --------- | ------------ | ---------------- |
+| `&` | `&amp;` | `&amp;` |
+| `<` | `&lt;` | `&lt;` |
+| `>` | `&gt;` | `&gt;` |
+| `"` | `&quot;` | literal `"` |
+| `'` | literal `'` | literal `'` |
+| any other character | literal | literal |
+
+`&` is substituted first, so an escape is never doubly escaped. The set is **closed**: exactly
+these four substitutions and no others, which makes the page fully determined by the collection
+and assertable byte-for-byte.
+
+Worked example — a bookmark with address `https://x.example.com/?a=1&b=2"z` and title
+`Tom's "A&B" <tag>`:
+
+```html
+<li><a href="https://x.example.com/?a=1&amp;b=2&quot;z">Tom's "A&amp;B" &lt;tag&gt;</a> <small></small></li>
+```
+
+Note the two differences between the columns above in action: the title's `"` stays literal while
+the `href`'s becomes `&quot;`, and the apostrophe in `Tom's` stays literal in both.
+
+A title containing a tab or a line break is emitted as stored, so such a title makes the output
+span more lines than there are bookmarks — accepted, exactly as in the tab-separated listing
+(spec Edge Cases).
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses (extended, not redefined — FR-017)
+
+| Status | Meaning | New in this feature |
+| ------ | ------- | ------------------- |
+| 0 | Success — including `imported 0` and the HTML page of an empty collection | — |
+| 1 | The store could not be used | unchanged; **never** used for an import-file problem |
+| 2 | Any mistake in what the user supplied | now also every import-file failure of FR-010, and an unsupported `--format` |
+| 3 | `delete` on an identifier that does not exist | unchanged; `import` never exits 3 |
+
+Results go to stdout, diagnostics to stderr, and a failing command puts no partial result on
+stdout (FR-018, `001:FR-023`).
+
+---
+
+## Internal contract: `src/bookmarks/`
+
+Not a user-facing interface, but the boundary the new code must respect (research R-106, R-109).
+
+| Module | Addition | Signature / behaviour |
+| ------ | -------- | --------------------- |
+| `importer.py` (new) | `read_records(path, shape)` | Returns a list of `{"url", "title", "tags"}` dicts, `tags` comma-joined. Raises `store.BookmarkError(msg, 2)` for every FR-010 cause. Opens no database. |
+| `store.py` | `add_bookmarks(connection, records)` | Checks every address first, then inserts all records in order, commits **once**, returns the count. |
+| `store.py` | `_insert_bookmark(…)` | The existing insert body, extracted and uncommitted, so `add_bookmark` and `add_bookmarks` share one path (FR-003). `add_bookmark`'s behaviour is unchanged. |
+| `cli.py` | `render_html(rows)` | Registered in the existing `RENDERERS` dict, which drives both dispatch and `--format` choices. |
+| `cli.py` | `handle_import(args)` + `import` subparser | Calls `read_records` **before** `store.open_store` (FR-011), then `add_bookmarks`, then prints `imported N`. |
+
+`store.BookmarkError` is reused for file failures with `status=2`, so `cli.main`'s single
+exception handler renders them with no new error path.
